## 2. Independently executed evidence gathered this stage

All captures are exact HTTP response bodies obtained via the admitted `public_https_get`, with sha256 capture identity. Per the tool's own disclosure, a captured response does not by itself establish semantic understanding; the quotes below are located excerpts read from the delivered bytes. Labels: source facts (S), inference for our plan (I).

### 2.1 Audacity manual — "Managing Audacity Projects" (AUP3)
- URL `https://manual.audacityteam.org/man/audacity_projects.html`, HTTP 200, captured 2026-10-06T08:11:05Z, sha256 `59c7f2d1620a96cbe81172b57ef012229b3f6d88d5889c91b60beb9cef5ee5f1` (37,614 bytes; first 32,768 bytes delivered inline, remainder in `public_captures/59c7f2d1….body`). Page renders under MediaWiki 1.28.2.
- S: A project is a single `.aup3` file; saving preserves tracks/clips/labels/envelopes/gain/pan, but the **undo history is not saved with the project** — history starts afresh on reopen.
- S: Two temp files (`*-wal`, `*-shm`) accompany an open project; deleting/moving them while open "will destroy the project"; they are removed on close.
- S: On close/exit the project is **compacted** (undo/temporary space removed) with a progress dialog; crash recovery is automatic on relaunch and "you may lose the very last thing you were doing."
- S: Trim is nondestructive — trimmed audio is retained but hidden, which can cause "project bloat"; there is no built-in command to purge hidden audio.
- S: AUP3 cannot be opened by versions prior to 3.0.0 (forward-compatibility break at a format migration).
- I (lesson for our data contract): a unitary project file that folds undo history and edit buffers into the same store creates bloat, fragile sidecar files, and version-lock. Our proposal should keep: original media **referenced by content hash**, an append-only journal for the edit graph, undo as derivable graph history (not stored audio), and a separate rendered-export store.

### 2.2 Audacity manual — "Error: Unsuitable drive"
- URL `https://manual.audacityteam.org/man/error_unsuitable_drive.html`, HTTP 200, captured 2026-10-06T08:11:23Z, sha256 `8e3697cfd5538151a7372b31254bcfba126cfa8762d73ab00c1a7d6b01e1b1e0` (18,298 bytes, complete). Footer: "This version created on 2026-09-02".
- S: FAT/FAT32 single-file limit 4 GB ≈ "about 3 hours 22 minutes of stereo at default settings"; Audacity **blocks** live projects on FAT/FAT32 and also blocks a temp directory there; edits/undo can push even short projects past 4 GB.
- S: Exports to FAT/FAT32 are allowed but size-capped (~6 h 45 m stereo 16-bit WAV); on failure "clicking OK will remove the partially exported file" — i.e. a failed/oversized export does **not** leave a file that looks complete.
- S: WAV/AIFF always capped at 4 GB, and the forecast size "can be accurately calculated in advance."
- I (lessons): (a) the two-hour-interview envelope must be sized with explicit numbers — at 48 kHz/24-bit/stereo float internal, 2 h ≈ 2.7 GB raw, so any bundle design that inlines rendered audio must respect a 4 GB portable-media boundary or split volumes; (b) "cancelled/failed ⇒ no artifact labeled complete" is an established best practice we should adopt: cancelled renders must delete or mark the partial file, and the export manifest must carry a completion record.

### 2.3 FFmpeg resampler documentation (libswresample)
- URL `https://www.ffmpeg.org/ffmpeg-resampler.html`, HTTP 200, captured 2026-10-06T08:11:24Z, sha256 `388a98a22ef074c5463f2c8660c7e53f0478ff3637d4ce291238ec2604533f42` (16,865 bytes, complete), `Last-Modified: Mon, 05 Oct 2026 16:22:41 GMT`.
- S: The resampler handles resampling, channel-layout **rematrixing**, and format/packing conversion; engines `swr` (native) and `soxr` (where available); `rematrix_maxval` "a value of 1.0 prevents clipping" during downmix; dither methods (triangular, Shibata, …) apply when reducing bit depth; `first_pts` allows padding/trimming at stream start; `exact_rational`/`phase_shift` govern phase accuracy.
- I (anchors for the time model): rate conversion is not position-neutral — an integer source-sample position maps to a non-integer output position, and compensation/padding options exist precisely because sample counts drift. Our contract must therefore carry cue anchors in **source sample positions at the source's own rate** and derive project positions by one declared, integer-exact rule, never store cues in project time only.

